        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An induction cooktop usually buzzes because the pan itself is vibrating in the magnetic field, which is most noticeable with multi-ply or disc-bottom pans on high or boost settings, and it is normally harmless. Clicking at low settings is the hob switching power on and off to hold a gentle heat, and a whirring sound is the cooling fan. What is not normal is loud crackling or popping from the hob itself, a burning smell, or noises paired with error codes, and those warrant a call to the manufacturer.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is It Normal for an Induction Cooktop to Buzz?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, in most cases. Induction hobs make sounds that gas and radiant electric cooktops do not, and nearly every manufacturer lists humming, buzzing, clicking and fan noise in the manual as normal operating sounds. They come from the way induction works: a coil under the glass creates a rapidly alternating magnetic field, and the pan sitting in that field is part of the circuit. Anything that can vibrate in that field sometimes will.
          </p>

          <p>
            The noise most people notice first is a hum or buzz from the pan at high power. It tends to get louder on boost, quieter as you turn down, and change when you add food or water. It also varies a lot between pans. A heavy cast iron skillet may be nearly silent while a thin tri-ply saucepan hums noticeably on the same zone and setting.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Induction Noises at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Buzz</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pan vibrating at high power</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Click</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Power cycling at low settings</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Whir</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cooling fan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Crackle</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Check if from the hob itself</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The useful question is not whether a sound exists but where it comes from and when. A noise that follows a particular pan, changes with the power level and disappears when you lift the pan is almost always the pan. A noise that comes from the hob even with nothing on it, gets worse over weeks, or comes with error codes, a burning smell or a zone that cuts out is something else.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of this page walks through each sound in turn: what causes it, whether it matters and what you can do about it. If you are shopping for quieter pans, our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best induction cookware</a> guide notes which constructions tend to run quieter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buzz" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Do Some Pans Buzz and Others Stay Quiet?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Induction hobs drive the coil at a frequency above the range most people can hear. The buzz you do hear comes from the pan's metal and layers responding to the magnetic forces. Pans built from several bonded layers, such as tri-ply, five-ply or disc-bottom pans with aluminum sandwiched between stainless, are the usual culprits, because the layers respond slightly differently to the field and to heat and can set up an audible vibration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power level matters most. The magnetic forces are strongest at the top settings and on boost, so that is when buzzing is loudest. Many people notice the noise when bringing water to a boil and find it fades once they drop to a simmer. A pan that is nearly empty also tends to hum more than one with food or liquid in it, because the contents damp the vibration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Construction details change the sound. Heavy, thick bases vibrate less than thin ones. Pans with a pressed-in perforated disc, a loose or slightly separated base plate, or a warped bottom often buzz or rattle more. A pan that does not sit flat can also chatter against the glass. If one pan is much louder than the rest, our pages on <a href="/can-you-use-stainless-steel-on-induction/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">stainless steel on induction</a> and <a href="/what-pans-work-on-induction/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what pans work on induction</a> explain how base design affects coupling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pan size relative to the zone plays a part as well. A pan much smaller than the zone, or one placed off center, sits in a less even part of the field and may hum more or heat unevenly. Matching the pan to the ring on the glass and centering it usually helps both the noise and the cooking.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is harmful to the hob or the pan in normal use. It is a characteristic of the technology, not a sign of wear. The only time pan noise is a warning is when a pan that used to be quiet starts rattling, which can mean its base is separating or warping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="click" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is the Clicking Sound at Low Settings?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clicking, especially at low and medium-low settings, is usually the hob cycling power on and off. Many induction hobs cannot run the coil continuously at very low power, so they deliver full or partial power for a few seconds, pause, and repeat to average out a gentle heat. The electronics, often a relay, make a soft click with each cycle, and you may see the simmering in the pan pulse in time with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is normal and is described in most manuals. It tends to be more noticeable on budget and portable units and on the lowest few settings. Some higher-end hobs smooth power delivery more finely and click less, but nearly all click at some point, particularly when switching on, detecting a pan or changing zones.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT IT MEANS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Rhythmic clicking at a low setting is the hob pacing itself.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the clicking matches a pulsing simmer and stops at higher settings, it is normal power cycling. Clicking with nothing on the hob or alongside error codes is worth reporting.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Power cycling can be a nuisance for delicate tasks like melting chocolate or holding a sauce, because the pan gets bursts of heat rather than a steady trickle. A heavier pan smooths those bursts out. Clad stainless or enameled cast iron hold heat between pulses better than thin pans, so the food sees a steadier temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may also hear a click when you place or lift a pan, as the hob detects cookware and engages or disengages the zone. That one is a single click rather than a rhythm and is entirely expected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Does My Induction Cooktop Have a Fan, and Why Does It Whistle?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The electronics that drive the coils produce heat, so induction hobs have one or more cooling fans underneath. The fan typically starts when you begin cooking, speeds up under heavy use and keeps running for a few minutes after you switch off to cool the components. A steady whirr during and after cooking is normal. Make sure any vents in the cabinet or below the hob are not blocked, since the fan needs airflow to do its job.
          </p>

          <p>
            A high-pitched whistle or whine is a different sound. It often appears when two adjacent zones are running at once, especially at high power or with pans of different materials or sizes. The two fields interact, and the difference between their frequencies can fall into the audible range. Many manuals describe this and list it as normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can often reduce whistling by moving one pan to a zone that is not paired with the other, lowering the power on one zone, or using pans of similar construction side by side. Linked or bridge zones that act as one larger zone can also whistle with a pan that does not cover both coils evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is not normal is a fan that never starts, a fan that grinds or rattles, or a hob that frequently shuts zones down with an overheating message. Those suggest a failing fan or blocked airflow, and running the hob that way stresses the electronics. Clear the vents first; if the problem continues, contact the manufacturer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="abnormal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Induction Noises Are Not Normal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some sounds are worth taking seriously. Loud crackling, snapping or popping from inside the hob rather than from the pan, especially if it happens with no pan on the zone, can indicate an electrical fault. So can a buzzing that comes from the cooktop body when the hob is switched on but not heating. Pay attention if a noise is new, getting louder over time, or comes with other symptoms.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sound</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Normal?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hum or buzz from the pan at high power</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pan layers vibrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rhythmic clicking at low settings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Power cycling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whirr during and after cooking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cooling fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whistle with two adjacent zones on</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Field interaction</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crackling or popping from the hob itself</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Possible electrical fault</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, stop and check</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grinding or rattling fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Failing fan or debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Warning signs that go with the noise matter as much as the noise. A burning or hot electrical smell, visible scorching, a cracked glass top, error codes that keep returning, zones that switch off on their own, or a breaker that trips are all reasons to stop using the hob. Switch it off at the breaker if you smell burning or see damage, and do not keep cooking on cracked glass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not open the hob to investigate. Induction cooktops run on high-current 240 volt circuits in most US kitchens, and the electronics inside can hold a charge. Diagnosis and repair belong to the manufacturer's authorized service or a qualified appliance technician, and opening the unit yourself can void the warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the hob is fine but you are worried about safety more broadly, our page on <a href="/is-induction-cooking-safe/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether induction cooking is safe</a> covers electromagnetic fields, pacemakers and burn risk in plain terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Make an Induction Cooktop Quieter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the pans. Heavy, flat-bottomed cookware with a thick, solid base is the quietest. Cast iron, enameled cast iron and heavier fully clad stainless generally hum less than thin multi-ply or pressed-disc pans. If one pan is consistently loud, it is often the one with the thinnest base or a disc that has started to loosen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn the power down. Buzzing is loudest on the top settings and boost, and most cooking does not need them. Use boost to bring water to a boil, then drop to a lower setting to keep it there. On induction you lose very little time doing this, because the hob responds almost instantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check flatness and size. Put each noisy pan on a flat counter and see whether it rocks. A warped base will hum and chatter, and there is rarely a fix. Match pans to the ring size and center them. If two zones whistle together, try moving a pan to a zone on the other side or reducing one zone's power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add contents before heating and keep some liquid or food in the pan, which damps vibration. Avoid heating empty pans on high, which is noisy and, for nonstick, risky; our guide to <a href="/can-you-use-nonstick-pans-on-induction/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">nonstick pans on induction</a> explains why. Small changes like these usually turn an irritating buzz into a background hum.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="service" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Should You Call for Service?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Call the manufacturer or an authorized technician if you hear noise from the hob itself with no pan on it, crackling or popping that is not coming from food, or a grinding fan. Call too if noises come with recurring error codes, zones cutting out, overheating messages, a burning smell or a tripping breaker. Write down the error code and model number before you call; it speeds up diagnosis.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the warranty first. Many cooktops carry at least a one-year warranty on parts and labor, and some brands offer longer coverage on certain components. A new hob that makes abnormal noises in the first months is exactly what the warranty is for, and an unauthorized repair can complicate a claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before booking a visit, rule out the easy causes. Try a different, heavier pan on the same zone. Try the noisy pan on a different zone. Clear the vents and make sure nothing is touching the underside of the hob in the cabinet. If the noise follows the pan, it is the pan. If it stays with the zone regardless of pan, it is more likely the hob.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most induction noise never gets as far as a service call. It is the pan, the power level or two zones arguing with each other, and it fades with better cookware and lower settings. If you are still deciding whether induction suits your kitchen, our comparison of <a href="/induction-vs-electric-cooktop/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">induction vs electric cooktops</a> covers noise alongside speed, cost and cleaning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the hob for pan noise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most buzzing comes from the pan, not the cooktop. Before calling for service, lift the pan and see if the noise stops, then try a heavier pan on the same zone. If the sound follows the pan, the hob is fine, and a flatter, thicker-based pan or a lower setting is the fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cooking everything on boost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boost and the top settings are the loudest and the hardest on thin pans. Most cooking does not need them. Use boost to reach a boil, then drop down; induction responds so quickly that you lose almost nothing. Your pans, your ears and your nonstick coatings will all be better for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blocking the cooling vents</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Induction electronics need airflow, and a blocked vent makes the fan work harder and louder and can trigger overheating shutdowns. Keep drawers and storage below the hob clear of the vent area and follow the installation clearances in the manual, especially with built-in models above ovens or drawers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using warped or rocking pans</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pan that does not sit flat chatters against the glass, heats unevenly and buzzes more. Check pans on a flat counter. Warped bases rarely flatten again, so replace the worst offenders, starting with the pans you use every day, rather than living with the noise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring crackling from the hob itself</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crackling or popping from inside the cooktop, particularly with no pan on it or with a burning smell, is not a normal induction sound. Switch the hob off, cut power at the breaker if you smell burning, and contact the manufacturer. Do not open the unit to investigate.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my induction cooktop make noise when nothing is on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A fan running after cooking is normal and can last several minutes. A click when the hob switches on is also normal. Ongoing buzzing, crackling or humming from the hob with no pan on any zone is not typical, and is worth reporting to the manufacturer, particularly if it comes with error codes or a smell.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do all induction cooktops buzz?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most make some noise with some pans, because the sound comes from cookware vibrating in the magnetic field. How much depends heavily on the pan and the power level. Many people find their hob almost silent with heavy cast iron and clad stainless at moderate settings, and noisier with thin multi-ply pans on boost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I hear the induction frequency itself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Home induction hobs typically run the coil above the range most adults can hear. What you hear is the pan vibrating, the electronics switching, the fan, or the interaction between two zones. Some people, often younger listeners, are more sensitive to high-pitched whine than others.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is induction buzzing bad for my pans?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Normal buzzing does not damage pans. It is just vibration at a level that is audible. The exception is a pan whose base is already loosening or warped, where a new or worsening rattle is a sign of wear. Check pans that change their sound, and replace them if the base no longer sits flat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my portable induction burner click so much?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Portable units often cycle power on and off to achieve low settings, and the relay clicks with each cycle. They also have small, fast fans that are noticeable. Both are normal. A heavier pan smooths the heat bursts, and running at a slightly higher setting with the pan partly off the boil can reduce cycling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do induction cooktops get quieter over time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The hob itself does not change much, though you get used to its sounds. What often changes is your cookware and habits: heavier pans, lower settings and better pan placement all reduce noise. A cooktop that gets louder over time, especially from the fan or the body of the hob, is worth having checked.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a different pan stop the buzzing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Heavy pans with thick, flat, solid magnetic bases tend to be quietest. Cast iron is usually very quiet. Thin multi-ply and pans with perforated pressed discs are often the loudest. Try a few pans you own on the same zone and setting to see the difference before buying anything new.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

